To develop a custom WordPress plugin that adds specific functionalities to an existing website, please follow these steps:

1. Define the requirements: Identify the specific functionalities you want to add to the website. In this case, the plugin should include:

- A custom post type
- A new widget
- Additional shortcodes

Make sure to define their specific features, options, and behavior.

2. Install and setup a development environment: Install WordPress locally (such as XAMPP, MAMP, or WAMP) or using a development environment like WP Sandbox. Create a new plugin folder in the /wp-content/plugins/ directory and give it a unique name.

3. Create a plugin header: In the plugin folder, create a new file named "plugin-name.php" (e.g., my-custom-plugin.php). Add the following code to the top of the file:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Plugin Name: My Custom Plugin
 * Plugin URI: http://example.com/my-custom-plugin
 * Description: Adds custom post type, new widget, and additional shortcodes to your WordPress website.
 * Version: 1.0.0
 * Text Domain: my-custom-plugin
 * Author: Your Name
 * Author URI: http://example.com
 * Domain Path: /languages
 */
```

4. Create the custom post type: In the main plugin file, add the following code to register the custom post type:

```php
function my_custom_post_type() {
  $labels = array(
    'name'                  => _x( 'Custom Posts', 'Post Type General Name' ),
    'singular_name'         => _x( 'Custom Post', 'Post Type Singular Name' ),
    'menu_name' => __( 'Custom Posts', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'all_items' => __( 'All Custom Posts', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'edit_item' => __( 'Edit Custom Post', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'update_item' => __( 'Update Custom Post', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'search_items' => __( 'Search Custom Posts', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'not_found' => __( 'Not found', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'not_found_in_trash' => __( 'Not found in Trash', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
  );
  $args = array(
    'label'                 => __( 'custom', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'description'           => __( 'Custom Post Description', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    'labels'                => $labels,
    'public'                => true,
    'menu_icon'      => 'dashicons-welcome-write-blog',
    'supports'              => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail' ),
    'taxonomies'            => array( 'custom_post_type_taxonomy' ),
    'has_archive'            => true,
    'rewrite'               => array( 'slug' => 'custom-post' ),
  );
  register_post_type( 'custom_post_type', $args );
}
add_action( 'init', 'my_custom_post_type' );

function custom_post_type_taxonomy() {
    $labels = array(
        'name'          => __( 'Custom Post Type Tags', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
        'singular_name' => __( 'Custom Post Type Tag', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
        'search_items' => __( 'Search Custom Post Type Tags', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
        'all_items'    => __( 'All Custom Post Type Tags', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
        'edit_item'     => __( 'Edit Custom Post Type Tag', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
        'update_item'   => __( 'Update Custom Post Type Tag', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
        'add_new_item'  => __( 'Add New Custom Post Type Tag', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
        'menu_name'     => __( 'Custom Post Type Tags', 'my-custom-plugin' ),
    );
    register_taxonomy( 'custom_post_type_taxonomy', array( 'custom_post_type' ), array(
        'labels' => $labels,
        'hierarchical'  => true,
    ) );
}
add_action( 'init', 'custom_post_type_taxonomy' );
```

5. Create the new widget: In your main plugin file, create a "widgets" folder and create a PHP file named "custom-widget.php". Within this file, add the following code to define your custom widget:

```php
<?php
class Custom_Widget extends WP_Widget {

  function __construct() {
    parent::__construct(
      'custom_widget',
      'My Custom Widget',
      array( 'description' => 'Adds a custom widget to your WordPress website.' )
    );
  }

  public function widget( $args, $instance ) {
    echo $args['before_widget'];
    echo __( 'This is my custom widget!' );
    echo $args['after_widget'];
  }

  public function form( $instance ) {
    if ( isset( $instance['title'] ) ) {
      $title = $instance['title'];
    } else {
      $title = __( 'New Title', 'my-custom-plugin' );
    }

    ?>
    <p>
      <label for="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'title' ); ?>"><?php _e( 'Title:' ); ?></label>
      <input class="widefat" id="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'title' ); ?>" name="<?php echo $this->get_field_name( 'title' ); ?>" type="text" value="<?php echo esc_attr( $title ); ?>">
    </p>
    <?php
  }

  public function update( $new_instance, $old_instance ) {
    $instance = array();
    $instance['title'] = strip_tags( $new_instance['title'] );

    return $instance;
  }
}

function register_custom_widget() {
  register_widget( 'Custom_Widget' );
}
add_action( 'widgets_init', 'register_custom_widget' );
```

6. Add additional shortcodes: To create custom shortcodes, create a new PHP file in your plugin folder, and name it "shortcodes.php". 

```php
function my_custom_shortcode( $atts ) {
  // shortcode attributes
  extract( shortcode_atts( array(
    'param1' => 'default_value',
    'param2' => 'default_value',
  ), $atts ) );

  // shortcode logic
  return '<p>My Custom Shortcode: ' . $param1 . ' and ' . $param2 . '</p>';
}
add_shortcode( 'custom_shortcode', 'my_custom_shortcode' );
```

7. Style and brand your plugin: To ensure that the plugin design and style match your website's branding and aesthetics, you can add custom CSS either by creating a new "css" folder within your plugin folder, or by adding it directly to your plugin file. You can style the plugin using WordPress' default styles, or by using a custom CSS file:

```php
function my_custom_plugin_enqueue_styles() {
  wp_enqueue_style( 'my-custom-plugin-style', plugins_url( 'css/my-custom-plugin-style.css', __FILE__ ) );
}
add_action( 'wp_enqueue_scripts', 'my_custom_plugin_enqueue_styles' );
```

8. Test and Debug: Before launching your plugin, run tests to ensure everything works as expected. Create a new post of your custom post type, place your widgets, and test your shortcodes.

9. Document your plugin: Create detailed documentation for your plugin, explaining its features, how to install and activate it, and any troubleshooting steps. This will make future maintenance and updates easier.

10. Finalize and Launch: Once you have tested, debugged, and documented your plugin, it's time to complete. Remove any unnecessary code, version your plugin file, and submit it for review on the WordPress repository.

Following these steps will help you create a custom WordPress plugin that fulfills the specified requirements, adheres to website aesthetics, and provides future maintainability.

To develop a custom WordPress plugin with the required features for an existing website, follow these steps:

1. Plan and define the plugin's features: 
   - Custom post type
   - New widget
   - Additional shortcodes

2. Start with creating a new WordPress plugin using the built-in functions:

   ```
   <?php
   /*
   Plugin Name: Custom Plugin (Insert Your plugin name here)

   */

   // Load necessary plugin files
   function custom_plugin_loaded() {
     // Load your plugin's functions
     require_once plugin_dir_path( __FILE__ ) . 'functions.php';
   }
   add_action( 'plugins_loaded', 'custom_plugin_loaded' );

   // Register custom post type
   function register_custom_post_type() {
     // Define your custom post type here
   }
   add_action( 'init', 'register_custom_post_type' );

   // Register custom widget
   function register_custom_widget() {
     // Define your custom widget here
   }
   add_action( 'widgets_init', 'register_custom_widget' );

   // Define custom shortcodes
   function shortcode_function( $atts ) {
     // Define shortcode here
   }
   add_shortcode( 'shortcode_name', 'shortcode_function' );
   ```

3. Implement the necessary functionality for each plugin feature specifically:

   - Custom Post Type (CPT)
     - Define the CPT with the necessary arguments such as label, singular label, and supported post statuses.
     - Define any custom taxonomies (if needed) associated with your CPT.
     - Add any custom fields (if needed) to your CPT using a plugin such as Advanced Custom Fields (ACF).

   - Custom Widget
     - Define your custom widget class extending WordPress' `WP_Widget`.
     - Implement your widget's rendering logic and add any necessary parameters.

   - Custom Shortcodes
     - Define any custom shortcodes required for your website, ensuring proper attribute handling.

4. Create styles or templates specific to your plugin features:

   - For custom post types:
     - Create CPT-specific templates to ensure a native-looking integration with your website.
     - Create a plugin CSS file and enqueue it to apply any necessary styles to your CPTs and widgets.

   - For custom widgets: 
     - Create widget-specific templates in a plugin directory or use a pre-existing method to create widget template files.

5. Test and debug thoroughly to ensure compatibility with the latest WordPress version and proper functionality.

6. Ensure appropriate plugin documentation, version control, and code commenting practices for future updates and maintenance.

7. At the end, consider releasing the plugin publicly on the WordPress plugin repository (optional) for other users to install and enjoy your creation. 

By following these steps, you will have developed a custom WordPress plugin with features that match your website's branding and aesthetic, using well-documented code and ensuring compatibility with future updates of WordPress.

