### Dependencies

To inject dependencies into Gauge test scenarios, we will use Spring Boot's `@ContextConfiguration` annotation. Here's an example of how to create a JUnit rule to provide this configuration:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import org.junit.BeforeClass;
import org.junit.ClassRule;
import org.junit.rules.ExternalResource;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;

public class MyTestContextRule extends ExternalResource {
    private static SpringBootTest.ContextConfiguration contextConfiguration;

    @ClassRule
    public static MyTestContextRule contextConfigurationRule =
        new MyTestContextRule("com.mycompany:invoice-management:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT");

    public MyTestContextRule(String contextConfigurationLocation) {
        contextConfiguration =
            new SpringBootTest.ContextConfiguration(contextConfigurationLocation, Arrays.asList());
    }

    @BeforeClass
    public static void setUpOnce() {
        System.setProperty("spring.test.context.report","json");
    }

    @Override
    protected void before() throws Throwable {
        Gauge.runWithDependencies(
            contextConfiguration,
            (dependencies) -> {
                Gauge.runScenario("my_scenarios", dependencies);
            });
    }
}
```

This JUnit rule configures the Spring Boot test context and uses the `Gauge.runWithDependencies` method to inject the dependencies contained in the Spring Boot application context into the Gauge test scenarios.

