```

Note: Not all GLB files may contain animations, so it's good practice to check first and handle cases where none exist. 

This will allow you to obtain the names of the animations associated with the loaded model. You can now use them to drive animations in your web application, depending on the framework you are using. 

For example, with A-Frame:

```javascript
loader.load('model.glb', (gltf) => {
  const animationClips = gltf.animations;

  if (animationClips.length) {
    gltf.scene.addEventListener('loaded', () => {
